Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 17:36:30 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-12-465] brave new mind

Anton Sherwood wrote:

>One limitation on meat that hasn't been mentioned in this thread is the size
>of the skull (or, if you prefer, the size of the adult pelvis).  Nano-logic
>will presumably be smaller than the old-fashioned kind.  So what I want my
>new brain to do first is _grow_ as it did when I was a child.  A growing
>brain is just the thing for learning new techniques.  (I haven't acquired
>another language since puberty.)

One thing I noticed today in some neuroscience paper: the author pointed
out that the size-increase in the human brain the last 500,000 years have
been very fast (on an evolutionary timescale). According to him, this
means that there must be fairly few and easily changed genes determining
brain size. Good hope for us! (and the pelvis-bottleneck could be
circumvented using an artificial womb). 

However, making an adult brain start to grow again is probably harder, at
least on a biological level. Especially since a lot of learning apparently
consists of whittling away unused neurons! Adding a lot of new ones or
making the brain more flexible may also make us more impulsive, irrational
and childish. Perhaps useful as a periodic therapy. The real goal must be
to find ways to add enough neurons to keep the brain in a good condition,
and use cognitive science to encourage more learning and development (i.e.
more of a software approach). 

