Subject: [#94-12-466] SING: Post-singularity morals

Peter C. McCluskey <pcm@rahul.net> wrote:

> I advocate the use of contracts to determine rights. Any entity that
>can agree to a contract and complain when it's contractual rights are
>violated should be able to purchase protection from a legal system.
>That way we avoid all the problems associated with psychanalyzing the
>entity to determine its intelligence, consciousness, or unity.

This definition has some problems with very alien entities or entities of 
dubious "mental health", where there are barriers of understanding or 
world-view. But otherwise its fine. 

>Except for components of a biological mind, there is a
>simple answer to enslavement issues; anything that can demand its
>freedom should get it, and many of the relationships that are internal
>to the biological mind should become commercial agreements for uploads.

This can create niches for some odd infomorphs: imagine a small 
computer-worm, which is able to demand its freedom (using a very small, 
specialized expert system or similar). It might not be intelligent, but 
it wants to be free! Or an entity which works as a "lawyer" for small 
infomorphs of low complexity, demanding freedom and protection for them 
in exchange for their services... I think post-singularity cyberspace 
will be a very weird place! :-)

>There is the potential problem that an intelligent being might become
>unable to earn enough to pay the rent (although that can be minimized
>via long-term contracts)

There are at least three kinds of necessities for infomorphs: memory
space, processing time and communications bandwidth/speed. Memory will
always be in short supply, because infomorphs can copy themselves faster
than exponentially. An infomorph unable to pay the memory-rent, might cut
down on processing cycles (like the "Slum Uploads" in Permutation City). 

The hardware constructors will probably be very important entities in this
society; perhaps like oil barons (but they only *add* more memory,
processors and communications, infomorphs can survive without additional
space). 

