From: Chris Hibbert <hibbert@netcom.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Dec 94 10:08:10 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-470] The Genetic Code Is Idiotic 

John Clark tells the story of Francis Crick designing an efficient
encoding for DNA, which turns out to not be the one that Ma Nature
settled on.  I'm not as surprised as John was that the truth turns out
to be more error-prone than Crick's design.  It makes sense for DNA to
evolve for evolvability.

Crick's design is too stable.  It doesn't allow for minor changes to
be slightly worse or slightly better than the original.  Ma Nature's
design copes with minor errors in transcription, and even in execution
and produces other working organisms.  Crick's system would detect an
error and then not know how to proceed.  MN just keeps going; as long
as it's a minor change in a minor subsytem, the effect will be minor.
Sometimes it'll be for the worse, other times for the better.
Variation in the replicants gives you some hope of surviving times
when the ecosystem changes out from under you, and since that's always
happening, adaptability for change is extremely valuable.

Crick's design requires that organisms be designed right from the
beginning.  If they're only close, they still won't move by a small
amount to a more suitable design.  The system is too stable.

Chris

